# 27. Security Requirements

The discovery system must:

- Treat all network responses as untrusted input
- Use strict parsing and bounded payload sizes
- Use short request timeouts
- Avoid generic port scanning
- Avoid executing content from device-provided names or metadata
- Sanitize strings before logging or rendering
- Avoid sending credentials to unverified endpoints
- Validate plugin identity before pairing
- Store secrets only in approved credential storage
- Redact sensitive identifiers in exported diagnostics
- Cancel stale tasks after network changes

Device names may contain arbitrary user-controlled text and must never be interpreted as markup or code.

---

# 28. Privacy Requirements

Aura must not upload ordinary discovery data.

The following should remain local:

- Device names
- Local IP addresses
- Host names
- MAC-derived identifiers
- Serial numbers
- Room names
- Service records
- Discovery history

Analytics, if introduced later, should record only coarse events such as:

- Discovery started
- Discovery completed
- Number of verified results in broad ranges
- Pairing succeeded or failed by integration category

Analytics must never include raw network identifiers or user-created device names.

---

# 29. Diagnostics

The discovery diagnostics service should report:

- Current network path type
- Local-network permission readiness
- Active discovery session state
- Session duration
- Providers started
- Providers completed
- Candidate count by source
- Verification count by plugin
- Verified result count
- Duplicate merges
- Provider errors
- Verification latency
- Cancellation reason
- Last successful discovery time

Example sanitized report:

```text
Discovery session: completed
Duration: 4.3 seconds
Network: Wi-Fi
Providers: 4 started, 4 completed
Candidates: 7
Verified devices: 3
Duplicates merged: 2
Provider warnings: 1
```

The normal user interface should show only actionable information.

